- in_text_citation: [(Hänninen, and Kramer, unknown_d)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/239007d37520659dd11b7452a76ac39fd4de54e5)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 189
[method]
The paper describes an empirical validation approach for operational models of the tree annual cycle. Controlled-condition experiments were used to isolate specific sub-processes, such as the temperature response of rest break and the effect of rest-break state on ontogenetic competence, while natural-condition observations were used for statistical fitting of whole-process models. For bud-burst models, the timing of the final bud-burst event was used as the response variable, and parameter values were estimated by numerical optimization. When conventional numerical estimation produced unrealistic parameter estimates, simulated annealing was used to search for parameter sets that made the selected model ecophysiologically realistic. This is related to the proposed method because the proposed climate-chamber cooling of saplings from April 4 to May 24 is also a controlled temperature manipulation intended to alter spring developmental timing and thereby test downstream effects on autumn bud set, similar in logic to using controlled temperature treatments to isolate developmental sub-processes in phenology models.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report quantitative experimental outcomes, effect sizes, or statistical tests for the controlled experiments. It only states that traditional numerical parameter-estimation methods failed in several bud-burst cases to yield realistic parameter estimates, and that simulated annealing was introduced as a method that avoids this drawback by finding an optimal parameter set for the selected model. Therefore, no numerical comparison to the proposed early-leafing versus late-leafing cooling experiment can be drawn from the paper.
[context]
The context is model development for boreal and temperate tree annual cycles, with empirical support drawn from both controlled and natural studies. Controlled experiments were designed to address specified details of modelled phenomena, such as the temperature response of rest break or the effect of rest-break state on ontogenetic competence, whereas natural studies lacked observations on all internal processes and relied on statistical fitting to end-point phenology. For bud burst, the process spans several months and multiple sub-processes, but testing was limited to timing the final bud-burst event. The excerpt does not specify sample sizes, chamber temperatures, photoperiods, species, or treatment durations, so it cannot be directly matched to the proposed design using 267 saplings, 2-7°C cooling from April 4 to May 24, and subsequent July and August cooling treatments.
[end related work 1]


