[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Marien et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0adc2794bfbe7c4a423efcde9763b4ade9e34922)
- venue: Biogeosciences
- citation count: 42
[method]
The paper used a manipulative glasshouse experiment on potted European beech saplings to test the effects of temperature and drought on tree stress and autumn defoliation. In 2018, 128 three-year-old Fagus sylvatica saplings of the same local provenance were planted in 35 L pots with a 20% peat and 80% white sand mixture. Eight saplings were placed in each of 12 climate-controlled glasshouses, and an additional 32 saplings were placed in four outside reference plots. From early May until 20 June, all glasshouse saplings were shielded with polyethylene film and received reduced irrigation of about 2.5 L once per week. Six glasshouses were warmed by 3°C relative to ambient air temperature, creating a combined drought, warming, and increased atmospheric aridity treatment, while the other six glasshouses followed ambient temperature and served as a less-irrigated drought control. The outside reference plots received abundant irrigation. Air temperature, relative humidity, and vapor pressure deficit were monitored, and soil water content was measured in one pot per glasshouse, although soil moisture sensors became available only after the drought treatment was alleviated. This method relates to the proposed research plan because it uses potted beech saplings in controlled enclosures to manipulate temperature and assess autumn phenological or mortality responses, but it does not implement the proposed pre-solstice and post-solstice day-versus-night cooling design, bud-set endpoint, or photosynthetic measurements.
[results]
The provided text does not report final quantitative mortality rates, bud set dates, or detailed phenological outcomes. It reports that the +3°C treatment had significantly lower daily soil water content and relative humidity than the +0°C treatment, with P < 0.001, and estimated differences of about 0.025 m3/m3 for soil water content and 20% for relative humidity. No significant differences in temperature, relative humidity, or vapor pressure deficit were found among the reference plots and the +0°C glasshouses. The experiment experienced significant mortality, which forced early alleviation of the drought treatment on 20 June. For later analyses, 20 saplings that showed early or abrupt defoliation before 18 August and 18 saplings with constant chlorophyll content index values below 3 from August to November were excluded, indicating substantial stress-induced defoliation or death, but no final survival, mortality, or phenology metrics are provided in the supplied text.
[context]
Key experimental conditions included 3-year-old local-provenance beech saplings, 35 L pots with 0.07 m2 surface area, 12 glasshouses with a 1.5 x 1.5 m ground area, and a polycarbonate roof that reduced incoming light by about 20% and altered ultraviolet spectral quality. The glasshouses had three openable sides and were equipped with humidity-temperature sensors; one pot per glasshouse had a soil moisture smart sensor, but these sensors were available only after the drought was alleviated. The outside reference plots were monitored with a pocket weather meter and received abundant irrigation throughout the growing season. Fertilization was applied once in April and once in July, using 35 g of slow-release NPK fertilizer and 1.8 g of microelements per sapling. The +3°C treatment raised air temperature by 3°C relative to ambient conditions, while the +0°C treatment followed ambient temperature. After 20 June, irrigation was increased to reference levels and the glasshouses were opened from July onward, with irrigation four to five times per week until the end of the season. This context is relevant to the proposed chamber-based day/night cooling experiment because it demonstrates controlled temperature manipulation in potted beech, but it differs substantially from the proposed method: the paper used warming rather than cooling, did not separate daytime and nighttime temperatures, did not test pre-solstice versus post-solstice effects, did not measure bud set, and included drought as a co-treatment rather than maintaining consistently adequate soil moisture.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Rodríguez et al., 2022-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/06adf12aa9442f7005344d40c1e823fd7a1ecdad)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 5
[method]
The paper used a common-garden phenotypic assessment that included phenological traits among 14 measured traits in Populus nigra ramets from 12 European provenances. Ramets were planted in a randomized 12-block design, and phenology was measured under common-garden conditions, although the provided text does not specify whether bud set was scored or measured with calipers. For statistical inference, linear mixed-effects models were fitted for each trait with provenance or bioclimatic principal components as fixed effects, garden block as a random effect, and tree height or cutting diameter as fixed covariates when correlated with the response trait. This relates to the proposed bud-set method and research plan because both use phenological endpoints and mixed models to separate treatment, provenance, or climatic effects from individual or microenvironmental variation, although the paper did not manipulate temperature or directly measure bud set.
[results]
For most traits, provenance and climatic origin explained less than 10% of total phenotypic variation. The largest reported fixed-effect explanations were tree height in 2019 and 2020, with marginal R2 values of 0.102 and 0.113, and flavonol content, with R2 of 0.199. Conditional R2 values were consistently higher than marginal R2 values, indicating that unmeasured common-garden microenvironmental variation among blocks explained more phenotypic variation than provenance or climatic origin. No trait-specific bud-set or phenological timing result is reported in the provided sections.
[context]
The study used 549 planted ramets, of which 433 survived summer 2018, from 12 sites spanning approximately 41 to 60 degrees N and -5 to 25 degrees E. Each site contributed 50 to 56 individuals except Spain, Poland, and Lithuania, which contributed 24, 27, and 12 trees, respectively. The common garden was located in Marburg, Germany, at 50 degrees 48 minutes 02.7 seconds N and 8 degrees 48 minutes 24.8 seconds E, at 328 m elevation, in a temperate oceanic climate. The garden was unshaded, fenced, weed-controlled with permeable plastic foil, and not treated with fertilizers, pesticides, or herbicides; ramets were watered frequently until the end of summer. Blocks contained 40 to 45 cuttings with 1 m spacing, and two ramets per ortet were planted where one was removed after establishment. Analyses used R version 4.0.3, lme4 linear mixed-effects models, emmeans for pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction, and marginal R2 calculated with piecewiseSEM. These conditions are relevant to the proposed beech experiment as a common-garden and mixed-model framework, but the paper’s species, system, and phenology measurement differ from the proposed bud-set caliper protocol.
[end related work 1]

