[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Bates et al., 2014-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9b536c7b0f45d4b512f337d0acda09ca3e4cd953)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 91285
[method]
The paper describes a penalized least squares algorithm for fitting linear mixed-effects models. For a supplied vector of covariance parameters, the algorithm updates the relative covariance factor, solves the normal equations, updates the linear predictor and residuals, and computes the profiled deviance or REML criterion. It uses sparse model matrices for random-effects terms, stored in compressed sparse column format, and avoids repeatedly forming dense random-effects products by storing the random-effects matrix or precomputing weighted cross-products such as XWX, XWy, ZWX, ZWy, and ZWZ. The output module then supports inference by estimating fixed-effect coefficient covariance, conditional random-effect variances, p-values, confidence intervals, profile plots, and fitted or predicted values. This method is directly relevant to the proposed statistical analysis because it provides the fitting and inference machinery for a linear mixed-effects model with treatment as fixed effects and bud type as a random grouping factor, allowing bud set timing to be compared against control treatments while accounting for repeated bud measurements and individual tree variation.
[results]
The provided paper sections do not report a quantitative ecological result from applying the method to bud set data. The reported outcome is computational: the penalized least squares routine returns the profiled deviance or REML criterion for use by a nonlinear optimizer, and the sparse-matrix strategy reduces repeated formation of dense random-effects products. No effect sizes, confidence intervals, p-values, sample sizes, or significance tests for treatment effects on bud set timing are reported in the provided sections.
[context]
The paper's context is a software implementation for linear mixed models, with inputs including fixed-effects and random-effects model matrices, the response vector, optional weights, and covariance parameters. It assumes a linear mixed model with Gaussian likelihood and uses sparse column-oriented storage and Cholesky-based updates. It does not describe climate chamber temperatures, tree numbers, or phenological measurement protocols. The research plan supplies the ecological context: Experiment 1 uses 267 potted European beech saplings in 10 treatment groups with early-leafing and late-leafing manipulation plus July and August cooling treatments; Experiment 2 uses 180 four-year-old beech trees in 9 groups with pre- and post-solstice daytime and nighttime cooling treatments. Bud set is defined at 90 percent of maximum bud length from repeated caliper measurements, and these data structures would be the empirical inputs to the linear mixed-effects modeling method described in the paper.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Gimbel et al., 2014-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3ef187885374e337148d90d0ddf0e34c0e35a3a8)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 38
[method]
The paper used repeated botanical and physiological monitoring of Fagus sylvatica saplings and understory vegetation in a field drought-exclusion experiment. At each of nine forest plots, ten 1 x 1 m vegetation sub-subplots were established and not entered during the experiment, with baseline surveys in June-July 2011 recording species identity and cover. For the beech phytometer component, 1080 one-year-old Fagus sylvatica saplings were planted in October-November 2011, with 12 saplings per each of 90 roof and control subplots. Saplings had initial heights of 30-50 cm and roots pruned to 10 cm; some were planted into resident vegetation, while others had a 20 cm radius of herbaceous plants removed to reduce competition. Saplings were monitored three times in 2012, in spring, early summer, and late summer, for leaf number, plant height, leaf length, and crown expansion. Relative growth rates were calculated from April to July 2012, and leaf stomatal conductance was measured on all monitoring dates with a leaf porometer. This method is related to the proposed Experiment 2 phenological monitoring because it repeatedly tracked Fagus sylvatica sapling growth and physiological state under an environmental manipulation, although it did not measure terminal bud length or weekly bud set.
[results]
The drought treatment did not produce significant first-year changes in phytometer growth variables. Only a small number of plots showed significant changes in leaf area index and species richness. Leaf stomatal conductance was reduced by the precipitation manipulation, with the clearest significant reduction in July and only a marginal significant reduction in September. No significant drought response was detected at the wettest site. Ecosystem evapotranspiration response to the treatment was observed only in the pine plot at the Schorfheide site during this initial phase. The provided excerpt does not report numeric effect sizes for growth or stomatal conductance.
[context]
The experiment was conducted in nine forest plots with paired roof and control subplots, including one 10 x 10 m main subplot and four 3 x 3 m satellite subplots per treatment. Transparent roof panels were adjusted monthly to target a 40-year return-interval drought, corresponding to relative precipitation reductions of about 26-33% at the three sites, while the roof construction itself intercepted 11-15.5% of precipitation. Air temperature and humidity were not altered by the roofs, and soil moisture deficits varied by site, depth, and distance from the central tree. The phytometer experiment used 1080 beech saplings across 90 subplots, with monitoring in 2012 after planting in 2011, and the drought intensity was proportional rather than absolute, which explains stronger responses in drier months and drier sites.
[end related work 1]

