[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Marien et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0adc2794bfbe7c4a423efcde9763b4ade9e34922)
- venue: Biogeosciences
- citation count: 42
[method]
The paper used a manipulative drought and warming experiment on potted European beech saplings to monitor autumn phenology, but it did not measure bud set. In 2018, 128 three-year-old Fagus sylvatica saplings from the same local provenance were planted in 35 L pots with a surface area of 0.07 m2, filled with 20% peat and 80% white sand. Eight saplings were placed in each of 12 climate-controlled glasshouses, and eight saplings were placed in each of four outside reference plots. The glasshouses had a polycarbonate roof that reduced incoming light by about 20% and modified UV transmission. From early May, glasshouses were covered with 200 µm polyethylene film and irrigation was reduced to about 2.5 L once per week. Six glasshouses were maintained at ambient temperature, designated +0 C, and six were warmed by 3 C, designated +3 C. The +0 C treatment represented reduced irrigation or drought, while the +3 C treatment represented combined reduced irrigation, warming, and increased atmospheric aridity. Reference plots received abundant irrigation. Air temperature, relative humidity, and vapor pressure deficit were monitored between 07:00 and 19:00 CET, and soil water content was monitored in one pot per glasshouse, although reference-plot soil water data were unavailable. Autumn senescence was assessed using chlorophyll content index measurements and defoliation status, with saplings showing early abrupt defoliation before 18 August or persistently low chlorophyll content index values below 3 from August to November excluded from senescence analyses. This method is related to the proposed Experiment 2 phenological monitoring because it uses potted beech saplings under controlled climate manipulation and repeated autumn phenology monitoring, but it differs from the proposed method by measuring leaf senescence and mortality rather than weekly terminal bud length and bud set.
[results]
The manipulative treatment produced strong drought stress and substantial mortality. The +3 C treatment had significantly lower daily soil water content and relative humidity than the +0 C treatment, with P < 0.001 and estimated differences of about 0.025 m3/m3 in soil water content and 20% in relative humidity. Mortality was high enough that the drought treatment had to be alleviated on 20 June by increasing irrigation to reference levels. From July, glasshouses were opened again and saplings were irrigated four to five times per week. For phenological analysis, 20 saplings were excluded because they defoliated early or abruptly before 18 August, and 18 saplings were excluded because their chlorophyll content index remained below the interpretable threshold of 3 from August to November. The provided excerpt does not report quantitative bud-set dates, chlorophyll content index onset values, or canopy-greenness onset values for the manipulative experiment.
[context]
The experiment was conducted in 2018 at Drie Eiken Campus in Wilrijk, Belgium, during a period that included an exceptional 2018 summer drought in the region, with summer precipitation of 134.7 mm, 20 rainy days, mean summer temperature of 19.8 C, and high sunshine duration and solar radiation relative to the 1981-2010 baseline. The experimental population comprised 96 glasshouse saplings, with 48 in the +0 C treatment and 48 in the +3 C treatment, plus 32 outside reference saplings. Saplings received 35 g of slow-release NPK fertilizer and 1.8 g of microelements in April and July. The glasshouse roof reduced light by about 20%, which was acknowledged as a potential drawback, although beech is shade-tolerant and preliminary tests suggested that the red-to-far-red light ratio at civil twilight did not change seasonally in the study area. Soil water-content monitoring was limited, and no soil water data were available for the reference plots. The method provides context for repeated phenological monitoring in potted beech under manipulative climate conditions, but it does not directly support the proposed weekly bud-length measurement protocol or the solstice temperature-switch hypothesis.
[end related work 2]

