[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Jean et al., 2023-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/15f902d1484767fe2c19854700d35d4a6f33d3e5)
- venue: Annals of Forest Science
- citation count: 6
[method]
The paper used a long-term, ground-based phenology protocol to record leafing, bud development, and leaf senescence on individual trees. Leafing was captured as a BBCH-based phenological stage assigned to the whole-tree crown, rather than as a binary leaf-out date defined by a fixed threshold such as 50% leaf unfolding. Two observers scored each tree from the ground using binoculars, reached consensus when scores differed, and checked the previous week’s score to avoid biologically implausible reversals. Observations were mostly weekly, and observer intercalibrations were performed before observation campaigns. This method is related to the proposed research method because both aim to monitor individual leaf-out timing, but the paper’s approach is observational and categorical in natural stands, whereas the proposed method uses a controlled potted-sapling design with an explicit >50% leaf-unfolding criterion to test solstice-related temperature effects.
[results]
The method produced 30,933 total phenology observations from 2006 to 2019, including 2,234 leafing observations: 1,530 for European beech on the north side, 73 for European beech on the south side, and 631 for silver fir. Graphical validation showed that bud development and leafing dates varied by roughly 50 to 100 days across trees, which the authors considered plausible given spatial, interannual, and individual variability along the altitudinal gradient. Silver fir showed later bud break than European beech, and leaf senescence began early in summer, around the end of July, consistent with environmental constraints. No treatment effects or statistical significance tests were reported because the study was observational rather than experimental.
[context]
Measurements were made on Mont Ventoux along north and south altitudinal gradients from about 875 to 1,545 m, with 772 trees total: 169 silver fir and 603 European beech. The dataset included 5,919 tree-year observations, with many trees observed for fewer than 5 years and a subset of 40 trees per species monitored long-term at two altitudes. Temperature loggers were installed in radiation shelters at 1.5 m height in eight plots. The setting was natural, not potted or climate-manipulated, and the protocol was designed to document natural phenological variation rather than isolate pre- or post-solstice temperature effects. Validation plots were produced using R version 4.2.2.
[end related work 1]

