[method]
The paper used individual-level spring phenology measurements of European beech, specifically time to budburst or leaf-out, in two settings: in situ on adult trees and ex situ in a common garden with seedlings. This is the method most related to the proposed approach of monitoring individual leaf-out dates defined as >50% leaf unfolding, because both rely on tracking the date of spring leaf-out for individual plants to quantify developmental timing. The supplied excerpt does not state whether the paper used a >50% leaf-unfolding threshold, the observation interval, or the exact scoring protocol, so those implementation details cannot be confirmed.
[results]
The reported outcome of the phenology measurements was that earlier budburst was associated with higher female fecundity and greater seedling growth, while drought stress produced contrasted effects on growth selection. The phenology data also supported the finding that male fecundity was constrained by mating opportunities and generated stabilizing selection on time to budburst. No numerical effect sizes, selection gradients, sample sizes, or statistical significance values are available in the provided excerpt.
