Processing method: Experiment 1 developmental timing manipulation to create early-leafing versus late-leafing trees: We will place some trees in climate chambers from April 4 to May 24, cooling them to 2-7°C with simulated day-night cycles, while others remain under ambient conditions.
Processing method: Primary phenological endpoint and measurement: We will use bud set as our primary marker for primary growth cessation and the beginning of autumn phenology, as it represents a key physiological transition point. We will measure terminal buds on primary shoots and lateral stems using digital calipers from July 4 to November 2. Bud set will be defined as the date when each bud reaches 90% of its maximum length, determined through linear interpolation between measurement dates.
Processing method: Experiment 2 day/night temperature manipulation: Pre-solstice treatments (May 22 to June 21) and post-solstice treatments (June 22 to July 21) will include four conditions each: chamber control (continuous 20°C), day cooling (8°C day/20°C night), night cooling (20°C day/8°C night), and full-day cooling (continuous 8°C).
Processing method: Experiment 2 phenological monitoring: We will measure buds weekly from August 25 to November 3 using the same methodology as Experiment 1.
Processing method: Statistical analysis: We will use linear mixed-effects models with treatment as fixed effects and bud type as random effects to analyze bud set timing, with effect sizes calculated relative to appropriate control treatments.
Processing method: Developmental timing measurement: We will monitor individual leaf-out dates (defined as >50% leaf unfolding).
